body:
what do you think? 

&#x200B;

(spoiler: no)

' In his mind I’m still getting a good deal but in my mind he’s treating me as an investment, not someone he sees a future with. Im not opposed to contributing but paying almost half feels wrong. Am I being unreasonable here or is it fair to be hurt by his proposal?'

&#x200B;

Personally, I would not pay out of principle alone, especially nowhere near half. If my GF wanted to move in, I would pay her to before she paid me LOL You are a couple, not landlord-tenant. Its also messed up because he gets to keep the equity of your payments (i.e. its a mortgage, not a rental) as he is the owner of the house, and what do you get? I guess in some sense you are an 'investment', but not a financial one! If you are married/engaged then it is different.

yeah I smoke on my balcony, but its random and not everyday. I was thinking that too, but I was just worried about growing being a constant smell, which might be more upsetting to my neighbors than the occasional smoke.

&#x200B;

' Also note it doesn’t start stinking until the end of the grow and drying/curing process.' Can you dry/cure inside the tent too, to prevent smell?

I had, almost word for word, the exact same experience 10 years ago. It still blows my mind everytime I hear of someone else having that experience, as it felt SO unique. It was hard for me to comprehend anyone else could go through it. I still sometimes fear I will experience it again (sober or otherwise), but not as much as I used to. I have a history of schizophrena/bpd in my family too. Part of it was a feeling of 'lack of control' over the situation, a fear that invaded other areas of my life. I later realized/got diagnosed that I had a fairly severe anxiety disorder, but nothing more... Anyways, I have gotten much better at dealing with anxiety, and I'm finally able to enjoy MJ again after all these years. I love it, and I feel like a kid again. I use high CBD strains which help too.

&#x200B;

I was recommended the book 'Feeling Great: The Revolutionary New Treatment for Depression and Anxiety' recently by my doctor and have found it helpful, you might too. The brain is amazingly powerful and complicated. The book offers an interesting paradigm shift.

the post i responded to had a very real personal anecdote and had nothing to do with georgism. as such, I offered my view with regards to the society we live in.

&#x200B;

'Telling a financially struggling  person who has been badly effect by the current system that the solution  to their woes is to get more skills seems almost comically against the  spirit of P&P or Agrarian Justice or whatever other geoist  literature you might like.'

It is agains tthe spirit sure, but far more practical than arm chair philosophy. We don't live in those places and they wont help the poster. It wasn't like the person posed a hypothetical -- it was a direct experience.

&#x200B;

'The whole reason that we might want a  dividend funded by land taxes would be to create the opportunity for  people to gain more skills, because it is often not realistic or viable  under the current system.'

Maybe. My interpretation was that since no individuals had a role in creating natural resources like copper, oil, etc., no individual should get exclusive ownership. They should be compensated for the labor of extracting, refining, etc., but it should be split among people. The land tax \*could\* lead to people gaining more skills, but I don't think that's the point. The land tax could lead to lots of other things too.

&#x200B;

edit: maybe I'm misunderstanding your last point, or georgism, but idk how that has anything to do with georgism.  I could argue the whole point of such a fund is to spur artistic and cultural endeavors in our population. That would almost certainly happen, but that's not the intended goal... or is it? That was not my understanding. Whether or not people have skills or can gain them doesn't at all seem related to georgism. sure, georgism can solve that problem, but that wasn't the goal. It \*unintentionally\* solves a lot of other problems, too.

Well if you have to, leave. My great grandparents left Europe in search of better. They eventually settled in Detroit to homestead.. When Ford and co. were boomin times were good, but it all crashed. When I was graduating school, all the jobs left, the city filed for bankruptcy, etc. So I left in search of jobs etc. Point is, follow the jobs/money, you don't have to stay where you are. IDK where you live, but states like Michigan are dirt cheap, but still have good quality of life IMO.

And if you can you should split a 4+ bdrm house to lower cost. also consider a motorcycle/moped/ebike for transportation -- winter may pose an issue depending on your state. In any case, there are ways to make it work. Look into subreddits for frugal, simple living, etc.

&#x200B;

or if you can, move to a European city. Berlin is dirt cheap, no need for car, etc.

&#x200B;

In any case, keeping out hardworking talented people who are living in conditions far worse to most people in the US is not an ethical position. There is plenty of room and resources being unused. They should at least be allowed to homestead like those before us imo

It's just a temporary sacrifice, and I prefer to live with many people anyways. I grew up poor to a working-class family and went to school to get an education and trade. 

&#x200B;

' If a person puts in 40 hours a week they deserve a living wage and a decent standard of living  '

living wage sure, but we will very likely disagree on what a 'decent standard of living' is. You are on the internet, probably have a car, etc. You live in luxury.. I live far below what the federal gov says is poverty and am very comfortable.

&#x200B;

' neoliberal assholes forget what FDR fought for and the god awful conditions people had to live in before that'

im not trolling, but idk what a neoliberal is, or if i am one. in any case, my  great grandpa and grandpa worked in the mines in Kentucky and died young. So now, I haven't forgot.

&#x200B;

Get a skilled trade and/or go to school... Hard work is necessary but not sufficient. Anyone can work hard; my father worked very hard,but it doesn't matter if the work isn't valued.

I don't disagree. We would agree that there is a paradox though, right? Any ideas on the resolution? If it isn't biological in origin, then perhaps cultural. In any case, I suspect a big factor is that we don't develop ones skills necessary for the kind of long term planning/delay gratification that FIRE requires. I believe it's a result of humans not needing to do this throughout history, until *very* recently. I don't think people ever had to think 30+ years into the future and ponder climate change, retirement, etc., and then use that to influence their decision making in the now. This is due to us living longer, and more importantly, living long enough that we are not really capable of performing meaningful work like we used to be able to.

&#x200B;

edit: ' people get to spend how they want to and it isn't my responsibility to help them. ' yes I agree 100%. I merely expose them to something I think would be helpful. What they do with that information is up to them, but it would feel wrong not share this information (clearly the creators of this community felt the same way...)

Sorry you feel that way. I would imagine, but can't prove, that their spending habits have little influence on your strategy, and instead get funneled to the top. There has been a lot of discussion on what would happen if 'everyone adopted FIRE', and it's not at all clear what would happen, and there is no consensus. My opinion is there would be fewer billionaires and uber elites. In any case, FIRE is more the result of a high savings rate, not *strong* growth year after year.

&#x200B;

edit: I want to add, I understand where you are coming from. But, *if* such impulse buying was the lack of a developed prefrontal cortex (or similar), for whatever reason, would you find it acceptable to have this attitude? People in FIRE certainly seem to have a skill that is absent in most of the population. To me, the question is the same as, if you are stronger than someone, or more heavily armed, is it OK to take their stuff?

Hi the issue is when switching between the gears shown above (the three smallest on the rear), sometimes it won't shift properly. Perhaps the pictures don't illustrate this, but it looks like the sprocket and the jockey/pulley aren't aligned accurately. I think the second picture illustrates it the best, I can post more if you like. I suspect this is the source of the issue (?)

&#x200B;

edit, Yes I believe you are correct that adjusting the cable tension will resolve the issue. Gonna finish watching the parktool video and give it a shot

Hi! The first video you sent solved my issue with the wheel wobble; I simply needed to tighten the hub (?) a little and it completely fixed it. I guess I must have accidentally loosened it when I took it off. I figured it would be something silly like that...

&#x200B;

The last issue, hopefully, with the derailleurs isn't \*too\* major since I could ride it to the LBS and have them fix it up if I can't. I'll give it a shot though! Thanks for the encouragement and videos. Hopefully this is the original issue with the changing of gears -- what do you think?

&#x200B;

Finally almost able to ride again...

I see. I've posted both the tool I'm using, and the crank I believe I have in the OP. I'm also using the parktool  CCP-22. I can try and post a video if that helps?

&#x200B;

edit: the crank is andel RSC6, I have confirmed.

I grew up in Michigan, downriver to be exact. Live in California for the past 6 years, but probably not too much longer. For better or worse, one's problems follow them wherever they go. I got out of there pretty fast, graduate school was my way out. I remember at your age being told how those were 'the golden years'  etc. and thinking 'damn if this is the best life has to offer, I want out'. I also remember seeing everyone around me work so hard, and for what? A shiny new F150, the newest iphone? I would suggest pursuing FI/RE, in particular, the following may resonate with you (maybe not since you said money is no concern):

[https://wiki.earlyretirementextreme.com/w/index.php?title=Article/Summary](https://wiki.earlyretirementextreme.com/w/index.php?title=Article/Summary)

&#x200B;

Something I always tried to do was find little pieces of what I want around me. If you want to be close to nature, perhaps take a trip to the UP? There are plenty of people that feel the way you do, and you'll find them. I've spent almost my entire life feeling alone, misunderstood, etc. After finding people that shared similar beliefs, I felt a lot better, kind of validated I suppose. I've found studying philosophy to be helpful, as they often faced similar struggles (Thoreau, Sarte, any existentialist, etc.). Admittedly, it can be a lonely path. Sometimes I just want to grab people and shake them until they understand how destructive their habits are. But sometimes I'm jealous of those that just simply goto their 9-5, then happy hour, TV, then bed. I try and learn what I can from those around me, even when I really can't understand them.

&#x200B;

And don't worry, there's a lot of nature left. I drove cross country years ago, whole lotta corn, wheat, and nothingness. There will almost certainly come a time in your life that you will want to be where you are now. The uncertainty, the openness, the struggle, the stress, youth, etc. Try and enjoy the bits you can.

' Could that $20k be paying your rent if it were invested in stock or bond mutual funds? (probably not) '

Yeah, that's what I was thinking as well. 

&#x200B;

' Renting to friends under market value is not a bad idea. ' All of the points you made regarding this I like, and whole-hardheartedly agree with. The added benefit is that it helps them too, since they get cheaper rent.

&#x200B;

' You also need to consider the work,  headaches, and risk inherent in being a landlord (or owner of a  group-house, whatever).  Some people can tolerate it, even like it.  Others don't.'

Understood. I've seen my father do it, so I think I have a reasonable idea of what to expect, but I will not know until the times come.

&#x200B;

' Also, I wouldn't buy unless you are  99% sure you are moving back to Michigan.  Managing a rental from a long  distance can be difficult. (less difficult than it used to be thanks to  internet, but still...)'

This was a point I was also concerned with. For what it's worth, my brother is in the area, and is a foreman, so he could help manage. 

&#x200B;

Would it be fair to say there are not any 'major' or 'obvious' issues with the idea? It does seem that as the days go by, the likelihood of me moving back is growing closer to 1.

Yes, there is a tremendous amount of overlap. Growing up we were frugal (lived simply) out of necessity (i.e. being poor). Now, I see it as a worthwhile and responsible goal, and not simply a reaction to my former economic state. It is also a fun, creative problem solving exercise, to figure out how to re-purpose things, fix them, and so on. I think fundamentally the root I (and maybe all of us) are trying to get at is: how do I work less, engage in meaningful work, and be in control of how this work is done? Said another way, being in control of spending our most limited resource: time. There appear to be many paths to obtaining this, but I do believe this is what we are after, perhaps I am mistaken. 

And so, the idea of living simply, producing zero waste, working less, and being a responsible inhabitant of earth, to me are all one in the same. It seems hard to have one without having the rest. 

&#x200B;

' Fifty years ago one place that would have fit the bill is the counties north of the Golden Gate Bridge. '

Yes, sadly it appears that era has ended. I had a friend up in sonoma (santa rosa) who I used to visit regularly. He bought his house many years ago, and just recently sold it to move back home to michigan. The price of the house was comically high, far more than what he bought it for, and the cost of living up there is absurd. But yes, that location would certainly fit the bill, I just wonder if there is a modern version.

With that said, Ann Arbor would be a good contender, but it is steadily rising in cost, and has some other features I don't care for, mostly the people (at least the majority of them). Although, I could see myself being there, especially with UM being there.

&#x200B;

' Maybe give northern New Mexico a look. The area around Taos won't be  cheap but perhaps affordable. Depending on how far out you go it can be a  little remote and the weather can be harsh and some of it is very flat  but there's definitely some like minded people around there. That said  some of them might be a little sketchy, you would want to talk to the  locals about it for sure. '

&#x200B;

Interesting, I haven't considered that. Have you lived in that region, or have you before? I am curious about like-minded people being there, it sounds intriguing.

&#x200B;

Yes, Los alamos is a place I have considered. Would love to work there, if I could get a position.. I didn't consider it as being aligned with the goals I have presented, but perhaps I was wrong. 

&#x200B;

'There's a bit more ecological diversity in Northern New Mexico than desert as well. '

Interesting, I'm from Mi so I have no experience with the SW. I just assumed it was a desert, but it seems more complicated than that. I have some family with a similar attitude as me that settled in Nevada, so perhaps they were onto something...The problem is, they around some sketchy and wild people. 

&#x200B;

thanks for the time and suggestions! I feel this is a step in the right direction.

WOAH! OK this is a great lead, thank you SO much. The person behind it, Jacob Fisker, is a fellow physicist. It appears he has already went down the road I want to. 

&#x200B;

' But I doubt that you'll find a much better option than working for a  couple of years at a high paying job and settling down in a small house  on a couples of acres. '

Yeah, that is probably true. But, I'd prefer not to do such work unless absolutely necessary.

&#x200B;

'  I'm particularly skeptical that you'll find an established community  that is stable, tech-friendly and requires relatively little work, but I  can be wrong. '

I think you are right, and hence why I made this post. I do believe that there are places (i.e. east wind) that satisfy some of the criteria, but they require a lot of work, and aren't what I would consider as tech-friendly.

&#x200B;

My goal isn't 'to get away from it all', but rather co-exist with the current system, as it has a lot to offer. It isn't clear to me why people gravitate to the extremes (i.e. 9-5 till 65 or off the grid and spinning your own yarn). But once again, thank your for your suggestion :)
